{% lede %}
As a graduate student in Jennifer Lewis's lab at the University of Illinois, I photographed printed structures for the group's papers. Features a few hundred microns across had to read clearly on a journal page. One of my photographs was selected for the inside front cover of Advanced Materials.
{% /lede %}

## The cover

The cover image shows a microvascular multinozzle printhead: branching channels that split one stream of ink into many parallel filaments. Against a dark background the channels glow and the geometry reads at a glance. The annotated versions carry the dimensions that matter to the science, so the same photographs work as both the cover and as figures.

## Figures

The rest are figures from papers across the lab: printed optical waveguides, pen-drawn flexible electronics, capsules for carbon capture, and antennas printed onto curved surfaces. Each needed a scale bar, clean lighting and a composition simple enough to read at column width. Where a photograph couldn't show the mechanism, I drew a diagram, like the cutaway of the capsule-making device.
